s you know belief system  is or what they hold to be true is  perfect either right it's just  impossible to get to that point where  yes i purged myself of all inaccurate  conclusions and false beliefs and i  everything i believe is 100 you know  accurate true and precise  that's that's an aspirational goal that  we will never get to the real difference  between people are those who engage in  metacognition and those who don't what's  metacognition it's basically thinking  about thinking it's turning a analytical  eye towards your own thinking processes  and trying to understand how you come to  conclusions how you come to believe  things examining improving tweaking that  process being open to changing your  beliefs based upon evidence and logic  etc and those people who don't really  actively engage in metacognition they  sort of go with their psychological  process level they're just sort of they  they're following the the current you  know the mental current to whatever  conclusion it brings them to  and not examining the process so you're  never going to be somebody who's  whose beliefs are pristinely perfect but  you can be somebody who examines  everything they believe and is willing  to change their mind and so again we say  it's a journey it's a process if you  live your life as somebody who can get  less and less wrong you know over time  that's the best you can do  but it's better than remaining in a  position where you're not even examining  your beliefs and you're just going with  with whatever your your id is telling  you to believe that that makes a lot of  sense last thing i want to talk about  changing other people's minds  there are many folks i've spoken to who  say  uh at the wholesale like at a societal  level when we see rapid changing of  minds it's often coming at the hands of  authoritarianism dictatorship the  chinese cultural revolution etc if we  think more at the retail level of  individuals it's building relationships  and often applyin