ief falls  eventually like everything gets  subjected to the skeptical the skeptical  eye  so that's the goal but that is  absolutely the long game that's years  you know it's a process you and even for  seasoned skeptics we still will fall  prey to emotional beliefs or  misinformation or not realize that we're  being ideological when we think we're  being when we think we're being logical  it's hard it's not it's not easy though  it's a complicated world and there's a  lot of misinformation out there  and our brains are really flawed you  know at doing this we we're only  semi-rational creatures  um so it's at best it's a process but um  you know if you engage in it either  personally or with other people trying  to to you know get them to be more  skeptical it's a lifelong process that's  interesting i mean thinking out loud  about an issue that we're dealing with  right now which is people blaming joe  biden for a lot of things that either  happened before he was president or that  are happening in other countries rather  than the approach of well what did he do  to cause that i wonder if there would be  something to saying hey listen do you  blame joe biden for the challenger  disaster and most people would probably  say well no i don't and maybe there's an  opportunity to say well why not and they  might give some reasons which then would  also apply to some of the areas that  they're blaming him today  yeah that's that's a good approach i i  like to take the approach  myself you know for myself but also to  other people it's like imagine if there  were a president of your party president  right now  and this were happening  what would you say  just do the thought experiment right you  just say oh yeah i would totally blame  them for everything just as much as i  would you really but think about it  really put yourself in that position  and just say you know if you had to that  and that's sort of the devil's advocate  approach like if you had to defend the  other position what would you say  a great always a great thought  experiment it gets people engaging with  the other side and then they and then  you know pretty soon like yeah you know  just getting them to think that way  makes them think that way that's the  psychologists have demonstrated that  frequently just if you tell people to  take to just pretend they have a  position they actually move towards that  position if you tell people just pretend  we're thinking skeptically here for a  minute  and then and then do that as an exercise  it actually gets them thinking more  skeptically that's super interesting  that that's a that's a very good idea uh  we've been speaking with dr steven  novella who is the host and producer of  the award-winning podcast the skeptic's  guide to the universe and lead author of  the book by the same name such a  pleasure having you on i really  appreciate it  a lot of fun thanks for having me
 

